# Files whose *bytes* are a contract, not just text.
#
# A minisign signature covers the exact bytes on disk, so any line-ending
# rewrite invalidates it. Without these rules, GitHub's `windows-latest`
# runner (where `core.autocrlf` is true) checks the catalog fixture out with
# CRLF and `verify_catalog` rejects it with the thoroughly misleading
# "the catalog signature does not match any trusted key" — green on Linux and
# macOS, red on Windows only (2026-09-28).
#
# `eol=lf` rather than `-text`: it pins the working tree to LF on every
# platform *and* normalises CRLF away on commit, so a fixture regenerated by a
# Windows tool cannot poison the repository either. The files stay text, so
# they still diff and review normally.
src-tauri/plugin-signing.pub text eol=lf
src-tauri/tests/fixtures/plugin-catalog/catalog.json text eol=lf
src-tauri/tests/fixtures/plugin-catalog/catalog.json.sig text eol=lf
